3
00:00:06,239 --> 00:00:11,914
The ostensible reason why Ahab did not go on board of the wher we had spoken

4
00:00:11,915 --> 00:00:17,914
was this. The wind and sea be token storms. But even had this not been the

5
00:00:17,915 --> 00:00:22,719
case, he would not, after all perhaps have boarded her, judging by his

6
00:00:22,720 --> 00:00:28,399
subsequent conduct on similar occasions, if so it had been that by the process of

7
00:00:28,400 --> 00:00:33,755
hailing, he had obtained a negative answer to the question he put. For, as

8
00:00:33,756 --> 00:00:38,638
it eventually turned out, he cared not to consort, even for five minutes, with

9
00:00:38,639 --> 00:00:43,359
any stranger captain, except he could contribute some of that information he

10
00:00:43,360 --> 00:00:48,799
so absorbingly sought. But all this might remain inadequately estimated,

11
00:00:48,800 --> 00:00:53,838
were not something said here of the peculiar usages of whailing vessels when

12
00:00:53,839 --> 00:00:59,755
meeting each other in foreign seas, and especially on a common cruising ground.

13
00:00:59,756 --> 00:01:04,878
If two strangers crossing the pine baronss in New York State, or the

14
00:01:04,879 --> 00:01:10,159
equally desolate Salisbury plain in England, if casually encountering each

15
00:01:10,160 --> 00:01:15,435
other in such inhospitable wilds, these twain, for the life of them, cannot well

16
00:01:15,436 --> 00:01:21,435
avoid a mutual salutation, and stopping for a moment to interchange the news,

17
00:01:21,436 --> 00:01:26,238
and perhaps sitting down for a while, and resting in concert, then how much

18
00:01:26,239 --> 00:01:31,435
more natural that upon the illimitable pinebear s and Salisbury plains of the

19
00:01:31,436 --> 00:01:36,799
sea. Two whailing vessels describing each other at the ends of the earth off

20
00:01:36,800 --> 00:01:42,638
Lone Fanning's Island or the far away King's Mills. How much more natural I

21
00:01:42,639 --> 00:01:48,159
say that under such circumstances these ships should not only interchange hails,

22
00:01:48,160 --> 00:01:53,359
but come into still closer, more friendly, and sociable contact. And

23
00:01:53,360 --> 00:01:57,838
especially would this seem to be a matter of course in the case of vessels

24
00:01:57,839 --> 00:02:02,878
owned in one seapport and whose captains, officers, and not a few of the

25
00:02:02,879 --> 00:02:08,079
men are personally known to each other, and consequently have all sorts of dear

26
00:02:08,080 --> 00:02:14,238
domestic things to talk about. For the long absent ship, the outwardbounder

27
00:02:14,239 --> 00:02:18,638
perhaps has letters on board. At any rate, she will be sure to let her have

28
00:02:18,639 --> 00:02:23,119
some papers of a date a year or two later than the last one on her blurred

29
00:02:23,120 --> 00:02:28,719
and thumbworn files. And in return for that courtesy, the outwardbound ship

30
00:02:28,720 --> 00:02:32,399
would receive the latest whailing intelligence from the cruising ground to

31
00:02:32,400 --> 00:02:37,594
which she may be destined, a thing of the utmost importance to her. And in

32
00:02:37,595 --> 00:02:41,755
degree all of this will hold true concerning whailing vessels crossing

33
00:02:41,756 --> 00:02:45,755
each other's track on the cruising ground itself, even though they are

34
00:02:45,756 --> 00:02:51,039
equally long absent from home. For one of them may have received a transfer of

35
00:02:51,040 --> 00:02:55,755
letters from some third and now far remote vessel, and some of those letters

36
00:02:55,756 --> 00:03:00,638
may be for the people of the ship she now meets. Besides, they would exchange

37
00:03:00,639 --> 00:03:05,518
the wailing news and have an agreeable chat, for not only would they meet with

38
00:03:05,519 --> 00:03:09,679
all these sympathies of sailors, but likewise with all the peculiar

39
00:03:09,680 --> 00:03:16,719
congenialities arising from a common pursuit and mutually shared privations and perils.

40
00:03:16,720 --> 00:03:21,594
Nor would difference of country make any very essential difference, that is, so

41
00:03:21,595 --> 00:03:25,914
long as both parties speak one language, as is the case with Americans and

42
00:03:25,915 --> 00:03:30,638
English. Though to be sure, from the small number of English whalers, such

43
00:03:30,639 --> 00:03:35,435
meetings do not very often occur, and when they do occur, there is too apt to

44
00:03:35,436 --> 00:03:40,159
be a sort of shyness between them. For your Englishman is rather reserved, and

45
00:03:40,160 --> 00:03:45,359
your Yankee, he does not fancy that sort of thing in anybody but himself.

46
00:03:45,360 --> 00:03:49,679
Besides, the English whalers sometimes affect a kind of metropolitan

47
00:03:49,680 --> 00:03:55,359
superiority over the American whalers, regarding the long lean Nantucketer with

48
00:03:55,360 --> 00:04:00,878
his nondescript provincialisms as a sort of sea peasant. But where this

49
00:04:00,879 --> 00:04:06,079
superiority in the English whaleman does really consist, it would be hard to say,

50
00:04:06,080 --> 00:04:10,958
seeing that the Yankees in one day collectively kill more whales than all

51
00:04:10,959 --> 00:04:13,999
the English collectively in 10 years.

52
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:18,159
But this is a harmless little foil in the English whale hunters which the

53
00:04:18,160 --> 00:04:23,119
Nantucketer does not take much to heart, probably because he knows he has a few

54
00:04:23,120 --> 00:04:25,594
foibless himself.

55
00:04:25,595 --> 00:04:30,399
So then we see that of all ships separately sailing the sea, the whalers

56
00:04:30,400 --> 00:04:35,999
have most reason to be sociable, and they are so. Whereas some merchant ships

57
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:40,318
crossing each other's wake in the Mid-Atlantic will oftenimes pass on

58
00:04:40,319 --> 00:04:44,878
without so much as a single word of recognition, mutually cutting each other

59
00:04:44,879 --> 00:04:50,558
on the high seas like a brace of dandies in Broadway, and all the time indulging

60
00:04:50,559 --> 00:04:55,999
perhaps in finical criticism upon each other's rig. As for men of war, when

61
00:04:56,000 --> 00:04:59,914
they chance to meet at sea, they first go through such a string of silly

62
00:04:59,915 --> 00:05:05,198
bowings and scrapings, such a ducking of ensons, that there does not seem to be

63
00:05:05,199 --> 00:05:10,159
much right down hardy goodwill and brotherly love about it at all. As

64
00:05:10,160 --> 00:05:15,119
touching slave ships meeting, why they are in such a predigious hurry, they run

65
00:05:15,120 --> 00:05:19,914
away from each other as soon as possible. And as for pirates, when they

66
00:05:19,915 --> 00:05:25,518
chance to cross each other's crossbones, the first hail is, "How many skulls?"

67
00:05:25,519 --> 00:05:30,479
The same way that whalers hail, "How many barrels?" And that question once

68
00:05:30,480 --> 00:05:35,198
answered, pirates straight away steer apart, for they are infernal villains on

69
00:05:35,199 --> 00:05:41,838
both sides, and don't like to see over much of each other's villainous likenesses.

70
00:05:41,839 --> 00:05:47,039
But look at the godly, honest, unostentious, hospitable, sociable,

71
00:05:47,040 --> 00:05:51,999
free, and easy whaler. What does the whaler do when she meets another whaler

72
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:58,399
in any sort of decent weather? She has a gam, a thing so utterly unknown to all

73
00:05:58,400 --> 00:06:03,435
other ships that they never heard of the name even. And if by chance they should

74
00:06:03,436 --> 00:06:08,719
hear of it, they only grin at it and repeat gamesome stuff about spouters and

75
00:06:08,720 --> 00:06:12,958
blubber boilers and such like pretty exclamations.

76
00:06:12,959 --> 00:06:18,318
Why it is that all merchant seammen and also all pirates and man of wars men and

77
00:06:18,319 --> 00:06:23,679
slave ship sailors cherish such a scornful feeling towards whale ships.

78
00:06:23,680 --> 00:06:28,399
This is a question it would be hard to answer because in the case of pirates

79
00:06:28,400 --> 00:06:32,878
say I should like to know whether that profession of theirs has any particular

80
00:06:32,879 --> 00:06:38,958
glory about it. It sometimes ends in uncommon elevation indeed but only at

81
00:06:38,959 --> 00:06:45,039
the gallows. And besides when a man is elevated in that odd fashion he has no

82
00:06:45,040 --> 00:06:50,318
proper foundation for his superior altitude. Hence I conclude that in

83
00:06:50,319 --> 00:06:55,039
boasting himself to be high lifted above a whaleman, in that assertion, the

84
00:06:55,040 --> 00:06:59,838
pirate has no solid basis to stand on.

85
00:06:59,839 --> 00:07:04,799
But what is a gam? You might wear out your index finger running up and down

86
00:07:04,800 --> 00:07:09,755
the columns of dictionaries and never find the word. Dr. Johnson never

87
00:07:09,756 --> 00:07:14,719
attained to that erudition. Noah Webster's arc does not hold it.

88
00:07:14,720 --> 00:07:20,318
Nevertheless, this same expressive word has now for many years been in constant

89
00:07:20,319 --> 00:07:24,719
use among some 15,000 trueborn Yankees.

90
00:07:24,720 --> 00:07:30,079
Certainly, it needs a definition and should be incorporated into the lexicon.

91
00:07:30,080 --> 00:07:38,238
With that view, let me learned define it. Gam noun a social meeting of two or

92
00:07:38,239 --> 00:07:44,399
more whale ships generally on a cruising ground when after exchanging hails they

93
00:07:44,400 --> 00:07:50,159
exchange visits by boats crews the two captains remaining for the time on board

94
00:07:50,160 --> 00:07:55,435
of one ship and the two chief mates on the other.

95
00:07:55,436 --> 00:07:59,435
There is another little item about gamming which must not be forgotten

96
00:07:59,436 --> 00:08:05,039
here. All professions have their own little peculiarities of detail. So has

97
00:08:05,040 --> 00:08:10,799
the whale fishery. In a pirate, man of war, or slave ship, when the captain is

98
00:08:10,800 --> 00:08:15,435
rode anywhere in his boat, he always sits in the stern sheets on a

99
00:08:15,436 --> 00:08:20,238
comfortable, sometimes cushioned seat there, and often steers himself with a

100
00:08:20,239 --> 00:08:25,119
pretty little milliner's tiller decorated with gay cords and ribbons.

101
00:08:25,120 --> 00:08:30,558
But the whaleboat has no seat a stern, no sofa of that sort, whatever, and no

102
00:08:30,559 --> 00:08:36,238
tiller at all. High times indeed, if whailing captains were wheeled about the

103
00:08:36,239 --> 00:08:43,198
water on casters like gouty old alderman in patent chairs. And as for the tiller,

104
00:08:43,199 --> 00:08:48,318
the whaleboat never admits of any such effeminacy, and therefore, as in

105
00:08:48,319 --> 00:08:53,119
gamming, a complete boat's crew must leave the ship, and hence, as the boat

106
00:08:53,120 --> 00:08:58,479
steerer or harpooner is of that number, that subordinate is the steersman upon

107
00:08:58,480 --> 00:09:03,435
the occasion, and the captain, having no place to sit in, is pulled off to his

108
00:09:03,436 --> 00:09:06,558
visit, all standing like a pine tree.

109
00:09:06,559 --> 00:09:10,238
And often you will notice that being conscious of the eyes of the whole

110
00:09:10,239 --> 00:09:15,119
visible world resting on him from the sides of the two ships, this standing

111
00:09:15,120 --> 00:09:19,999
captain is all alive to the importance of sustaining his dignity by maintaining

112
00:09:20,000 --> 00:09:26,079
his legs. Nor is this any very easy matter, for in his rear is the immense

113
00:09:26,080 --> 00:09:30,958
projecting steering ore hitting him now and then in the small of his back, the

114
00:09:30,959 --> 00:09:36,719
aftere reciprocating by wrapping his knees in front. He is thus completely

115
00:09:36,720 --> 00:09:42,238
wedged before and behind, and can only expand himself sideways by settling down

116
00:09:42,239 --> 00:09:47,518
on his stretched legs. But a sudden, violent pitch of the boat will often go

117
00:09:47,519 --> 00:09:51,838
far to topple him, because length of foundation is nothing without

118
00:09:51,839 --> 00:09:56,958
corresponding breadth. Merely make a spread angle of two poles, and you

119
00:09:56,959 --> 00:10:02,399
cannot stand them up. Then again, it would never do in plain sight of the

120
00:10:02,400 --> 00:10:07,518
world's riveted eyes. It would never do, I say, for this straddling captain to be

121
00:10:07,519 --> 00:10:12,238
seen steadying himself the slightest particle by catching hold of anything

122
00:10:12,239 --> 00:10:18,638
with his hands. Indeed, as token of his entire buoyant self-comand, he generally

123
00:10:18,639 --> 00:10:23,518
carries his hands in his trousers pockets, but perhaps being generally

124
00:10:23,519 --> 00:10:28,878
very large, heavy hands, he carries them there for ballast. Nevertheless, there

125
00:10:28,879 --> 00:10:32,958
have occurred instances, well authenticated ones too, where the

126
00:10:32,959 --> 00:10:37,518
captain has been known for an uncommonly critical moment or two in a sudden

127
00:10:37,519 --> 00:10:47,400
squall, say, to seize hold of the nearest orarssman's hair and hold on there like grim death.
